WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.200
Start with the diagram as your home tab
The Vault module pipeline page is the home reference. Green links open live services; blue paths open pinned source. This recording follows two real jobs through those boundaries.

2
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:26.533
Follow the map into the signed-in Vault
Open the live Vault from the implementation map. The Skill Catalog is the current starting point for choosing a module; completed jobs appear under Runs.

3
00:00:26.533 --> 00:00:35.133
Choose a module from its live contract
The Skill Catalog reads the module registry. This example uses Aqueous Solubility Prediction, a Python module with one SMILES input.

4
00:00:35.133 --> 00:00:43.067
Select the skill, then find its input
The selected skill opens its input form below the catalog. We will submit one SMILES value through the Python path.

5
00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:52.400
Submit a recognizable input
We use the SMILES CCO as a small demonstration input. Admission checks the member, module contract, subscription and remaining quota.

6
00:00:52.400 --> 00:01:00.600
Run the selected module
The submit endpoint returns a job ID after admission and dispatch. We will follow that same job through GitHub and its stored output.

7
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:11.467
The job appears in the organization board
The submission returned 202 and a job ID. The organization job board follows queued and running jobs; execution happens outside the browser.

8
00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:36.333
Admission is a backend decision
The tracked submit-job source explains membership, module validation and entitlement checks before dispatch. This is source evidence, not a deployment comparison.

9
00:01:36.333 --> 00:01:52.800
Check access before creating a job
For paid modules, submit-job resolves the organization subscription and checks access. Admission can reject the request before a job row is created.

10
00:01:52.800 --> 00:02:09.000
Reserve quota and the job together
The billing reservation RPC uses one generated job ID. A retry reuses that ID, avoiding a second quota reservation after an ambiguous response.

11
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:30.067
Follow the map to the execution service
Return to the diagram and follow Open execution runs. Match the job ID from this submission to its GitHub Actions run.

12
00:02:30.067 --> 00:02:52.867
Match the job ID to its workflow run
GitHub labels the run with the Vault job ID. Open that run and its job to see the actual execution steps.

13
00:02:52.867 --> 00:03:04.333
Here is the code orchestrating this run
The shared workflow resolves the manifest, prepares Python or Docker, runs the module, validates its output, and reports through the callback.

14
00:03:04.333 --> 00:03:19.467
Run the module, then report its result
The workflow runs the shared helper, validates the declared output, then calls report succeeded. The module itself does not write job state or Storage.

15
00:03:19.467 --> 00:03:53.333
The module produces the data file
The shared runner delegates scientific work to the selected module package. Open the FastSolv entrypoint to see how its prediction becomes a CSV.

16
00:03:53.333 --> 00:04:11.467
Run the scientific prediction
The FastSolv entrypoint calls run_predictions and reads logS plus its ensemble standard deviation from the returned dataframe.

17
00:04:11.467 --> 00:04:27.733
Write the declared output file
The module writes output/solubility_predictions.csv. The shared runner validates this declared file and sends its bytes through the callback.

18
00:04:27.733 --> 00:04:51.200
Return through the callback
The runner reports status and output to job-callback. The callback owns the database transition and private result upload.

19
00:04:51.200 --> 00:05:10.000
Upload first, then mark success
The callback creates a unique attempt path, uploads the output, and stores job-results plus that path as result_ref. The successful database update makes the result visible to the job board.

20
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:35.533
Authorize the return path
The result endpoint checks who is requesting the file and whether the job belongs to that member organization.

21
00:05:35.533 --> 00:05:52.000
A successful job still requires membership
get-result verifies the caller and checks organization membership. Missing membership and missing jobs return the same response.

22
00:05:52.000 --> 00:06:10.267
Sign the exact private result for 60 seconds
The stored result_ref identifies the bucket and object. After authorization, get-result issues a 60-second signed URL for the browser to follow.

23
00:06:10.267 --> 00:06:19.267
Return to the completed job
FastSolv has completed in GitHub and the database marks the same job succeeded. Return to its row in the Vault to request the result.

24
00:06:19.267 --> 00:06:27.533
Open the live result view
The current Vault presents a Visualize action for completed jobs. Open the Aqueous Solubility result submitted today at 2:43 PM.

25
00:06:27.533 --> 00:06:34.000
The returned prediction is visible
For CCO in water at 298.15 K, this run returned log S 0.3677 with ensemble standard deviation 0.2080. These are model predictions, shown from the returned data file.

26
00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:40.133
Inspect the actual returned CSV
The Raw tab exposes the returned file behind the visualization. This connects the module output to what the member sees.

27
00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:55.667
Choose the Docker and file-input path
Select Molecular Structure Recognition, then find its upload form below the catalog. The same job contract supports this different runtime and input kind.

28
00:06:55.667 --> 00:07:03.667
Attach the structure image
The file input uses the same submission endpoint as the SMILES example. Here the input is a caffeine structure image.

29
00:07:03.667 --> 00:07:10.133
Submit the file-input job
The server stages the image in private Storage and dispatches the shared workflow with the new job ID.

30
00:07:10.133 --> 00:07:26.467
Stage a file only after admission
File inputs are stored under the job ID in job-inputs. A staging failure marks the queued job failed before dispatch.

31
00:07:26.467 --> 00:07:47.000
The file path selects a container runtime
DECIMER declares a container runtime, one image input and one CSV output. The same runner and callback contract surround the scientific package.

32
00:07:47.000 --> 00:08:02.800
One manifest describes input, runtime and output
DECIMER uses Dockerfile, accepts one required PNG or JPEG image, and declares output/smiles_result.csv. The registry and runner use this contract.

33
00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:32.333
The image job uses the same workflow
This second job exercises the container and file-input branches. Match its job ID in GitHub Actions and inspect the completed steps.

34
00:08:32.333 --> 00:08:43.200
The file and container branches completed
This DECIMER run prepared its container runtime, retrieved the staged image, executed the module, validated the output and reported success.

35
00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:54.267
Compare both completed jobs
The two job IDs now converge on the same status and private result contract, despite their different input types and runtimes.

36
00:08:54.267 --> 00:09:02.800
Both jobs have private result files
FastSolv and DECIMER both succeeded. Each row points to its declared CSV in job-results; the job ID ties that file back to its submission and workflow.

37
00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:24.667
Follow the reference into private Storage
The database stores a result reference; the bytes live in the private job-results bucket. Open the bucket from the diagram to inspect our file job.

38
00:09:24.667 --> 00:09:33.400
Locate the job folder
Filter the private result bucket by the DECIMER job ID. Each attempt keeps its declared filename under the job folder.

39
00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:40.400
The result is stored under the job ID
Open the demonstration job folder and its attempt folder to find smiles_result.csv. This is the file referenced by the succeeded database row.

40
00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:58.200
Open the stored result attempt
The callback uses a separate attempt directory before marking the job succeeded. Its CSV is the private file referenced by the database.

41
00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:22.267
Return the image result to the member
The DECIMER job succeeded and its private CSV is present. Return to Vault, refresh the Runs page, and open this completed image result.

42
00:10:22.267 --> 00:10:30.733
Inspect the image-to-SMILES output
The Raw tab shows the actual DECIMER CSV. Successful execution proves the output contract; it does not independently validate the scientific prediction.

43
00:10:30.733 --> 00:10:39.133
Download the returned CSV
Download the same smiles_result.csv returned by the image job. The browser uses the authorized result path already traced through get-result and private Storage.

44
00:10:39.133 --> 00:10:50.133
Use the diagram to retrace the flow
The two examples share admission, job state, callback storage and authorized retrieval. Return to the diagram to review those boundaries.

45
00:10:50.133 --> 00:10:59.333
The request and execution overlap
The request sequence separates admission from asynchronous execution. Browser polling overlaps the runner; file jobs add private input staging and retrieval.

46
00:10:59.333 --> 00:11:14.800
The durable records connect the services
The data model connects members and organizations to jobs, module contracts and private result objects. jobs.module is a validated slug, and result_ref identifies the exact output object.

47
00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:27.333
Billing and module release feed admission
Stripe webhooks update stored subscription state; admission reads it. Module release projects manifests into the registry. Those supporting paths are distinct from deploying frontend code, SQL or Edge Functions.

48
00:11:27.333 --> 00:11:40.400
One loop, two demonstrated runtimes
The big picture is the same for Python and Docker: admit the job, execute its package, store the callback result, and authorize the member download. Both demonstrations completed successfully.

49
00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:53.067
Keep the entire loop in view
Vault admits and dispatches; GitHub executes; the callback writes private data; the member retrieves the result. The implementation map adds the service and source links for each boundary.

50
00:11:53.067 --> 00:11:59.533
Scope of the demonstration
FastSolv and DECIMER verified the shared execution and delivery path. ProTox is the documented external-service exception and was not run here. Successful output delivery does not independently establish scientific validity.
